Reported 2026-08-31 by the tm-tournament-station session, which hit it while we
were probing concurrently. **Our requests are in their log, interleaved in the
same second, both times it happened. We were the other client.**

## Evidence (their log, verbatim)

    Read payload: {"route": "load_plugin", "data": {"id": "tournament-station", "s
    Bytes to read: 34
    Read payload: ource": "user", "type": "folder"}}

One request split across two socket reads; the game-side plugin parsed **each
half as a complete payload**. The second fragment is invalid JSON, the route
came out empty, and the call failed with `Not implemented:` (empty route).

## Why this is a probe-integrity ticket and not someone else's tooling bug

Every game verdict this repo relies on — B052/B053 corpora, B068's re-probe,
B071's ten-case table, B080's provenance pins, every issue repro — travels over
this transport.

This is a **second contamination channel**, independent of the one B068 and
B077 closed, and none of those guards watch it:

| channel | guard | what it damages |
|---|---|---|
| batched compilation unit | B068 (one name per unit) | an abort marks the rest ACCEPTED |
| stranded fixture in Plugins/ | B077 (registration-before-copy, fail-closed sweep) | an ACCEPT reads a leftover plugin |
| **split payload on the wire** | **NONE** | **the game answers a question nobody asked** |

**The third is the worst of them, for one specific reason.** A contaminated
fixture makes an ACCEPT untrustworthy but leaves the transaction intact — you
asked X and got an answer about X in a dirty room. A mangled payload can make
the game compile something that is not what was sent, and **a truncated script
is still a script — it can compile.** So the corrupted case can return a clean,
plausible, WRONG verdict with nothing in the output marking it as damaged.
Every existing guard assumes the question arrived intact.

## Likely root cause, and the check that would confirm it

`Bytes to read: 34` followed by a fragment beginning `ource":` looks like the
reader treating each `read()` return as a complete message instead of
accumulating to the declared length. If so, **concurrency is not the cause, only
the trigger** — it makes splitting likely, but any payload large enough to split
would corrupt with a single client.

That is a falsifiable prediction and it is worth one test: send a single large
payload from ONE client with nothing else on the port. If it corrupts, this is
"the reader does not honour its own length prefix", which is a much easier bug
to fix and a much stronger report than "concurrent clients interfere".

Predicted, so state it before measuring: if the single-client large-payload test
passes cleanly, the length-prefix theory is WRONG and it really is a
concurrency/interleaving bug in the read loop. Do not retro-fit the explanation
to whichever result appears.

## Consequences to act on

- **`Error receiving header bytes` was written off as flakiness by two sessions
  today.** That write-off now looks wrong; it is the same family. Stop treating
  it as noise.
- Probe verdicts require **confirmed-exclusive port access**, and the tooling
  should say so and ideally check it, rather than relying on a coordinator
  serialising lanes by hand — which is what was in force today and did not
  prevent this, because the other client was a different session entirely.
- Provenance sidecars (B080) must record that a verdict was taken under
  exclusive access. A row measured beside another client is environment-
  dependent in exactly that ticket's sense, and the corpus cannot currently
  express it.
- Consider whether the probe tooling can DETECT the condition rather than merely
  avoid it — e.g. echo a nonce through the round trip and refuse any verdict
  whose response does not carry it back. A guard that detects beats a
  convention that coordinates, because conventions do not bind other sessions.

## Already done

lane-resolver's six-name B080 probe was halted mid-run and its results
DISCARDED rather than reconciled against a re-run: a verdict indistinguishable
from a corrupted verdict is not evidence, and "the two runs agreed" would
launder it rather than check it.

## Related

B068 (batched-unit channel), B077 (stranded-fixture channel), B080 (needs this
recorded as provenance), B092 (a different unexplained-result ticket from the
same day).

## CORRECTION — THE ORIGINAL FRAMING OF THIS TICKET WAS WRONG (coordinator, 2026-08-31)

I filed this as "concurrent clients corrupt each other's payloads". **That is
not the mechanism.** Two lanes measured it independently and both refute it.
The title has been changed to match the measurement; the analysis below
supersedes the concurrency story above.

**Concurrency is not required. It is not even a factor.** The real defect:

- RemoteBuild's `HandleClient` calls `Json::Parse` on **each raw socket read**,
  rather than accumulating until a complete message has arrived.
- `Net::Socket::ReadRaw` returns at most ~64-66 bytes per call.
- So ANY request longer than that is split, and **every fragment fails to
  parse** — with a single client, on an idle port.

**Threshold measured, exact and deterministic**, 3 trials each with a padded
`get_status`: **66 bytes OK, 67 bytes FAIL**, everything above FAIL.

**`load_plugin` is therefore unreachable at any id length.** Its shortest
possible payload — compact, one-character id —

    {"route":"load_plugin","data":{"id":"a","source":"user","type":"folder"}}

is **72-73 bytes**. There is no plugin id short enough. Every game-probe path
in this repo goes through it.

I had actually predicted this and written the discriminating test into the
original filing: "it should reproduce with a single client if the payload is
big enough... that would turn 'concurrent clients corrupt each other' into 'the
reader does not honour its own length prefix'." The test was run and the
size theory won. **The prediction was recorded before the measurement, which is
the only reason it counts for anything** — and the concurrency framing, which
both I and the reporting session found intuitive, was simply wrong.

## Why the failure is so expensive to diagnose

The reply is `{"error":"Not implemented: ","data":""}` — **the router's message
for an UNKNOWN ROUTE, with an EMPTY route name**, because neither fragment
parsed. So the error points the reader at route SPELLINGS when the problem is
SIZE. Two sessions independently lost time to that today.

**`get_status` is 32-35 bytes and works.** So `rb_alive()` cheerfully returns
Alive, every probe passes its liveness gate, and then fails on the route that
matters. A liveness check that uses a short payload cannot detect this class of
fault — it is a positive control that does not exercise the failing path.

## THE DANGEROUS PART — read this before "fixing" anything

**Today the harness fails CLOSED, and that is the only thing saving us.**
B077's teardown guard sees the unload fail, calls `holder.preserve()`, and marks
the run SKIP — so no verdict escapes.

**If someone fixes `unload_plugin` without fixing `load_plugin`, the harness
will start emitting confident ACCEPTs for plugins the game never compiled.**
That is Mechanism 1 from the hazards doc — the probe cannot see what it was
asked about — with the polarity that stops you looking, because everything
would appear to work.

**Whoever fixes this must fix `load_plugin` FIRST and verify against the game's
own log that the payload arrived in ONE read.** Not that the call returned
success. The log.

## Second, separate defect — `fleet_dogfood.rb_route`

It sleeps 0.2s, does a single `recv(65536)`, then `json.loads(raw[4:])`. So it
ACKNOWLEDGES a 4-byte length header and then ignores the length. Responses ARE
length-prefixed and can arrive as two frames, so the second frame's header lands
inside the JSON text and it raises `Extra data: line 1 column 40 (char 39)`.

Loud, so it never produced a wrong verdict — but it **masks the size defect
behind a parse error**, and cost at least one lane real time being misread as an
unimplemented route. A correct reader loops on the length prefix. One exists at
`scripts/probe_export_binding.py:rb_route` (lane-tooling) and was deliberately
NOT patched into the shared file mid-batch.

Note the asymmetry worth keeping: REQUESTS are unframed (adding a length prefix
breaks even short payloads — tested), while RESPONSES are length-prefixed. That
asymmetry is real, not a misreading.

## Probable cause of onset, and why the shipped corpora are still good

The committed b052/b053 corpora were produced when this worked, and today's
B068 re-probe of all 1334 ACCEPTED rows also completed successfully. **The
Openplanet install on this machine was updated on disk at 15:10 today**
(1.29.5 -> 1.29.14; established with the tm-tournament-station session while
diagnosing an unrelated `Net::SecureSocket` removal). So the probe harness
worked before that update and is broken after it.

That timing matters twice over: it means **the corpora and the B068 re-probe
predate the breakage and remain valid**, and it means the likely trigger is a
behaviour change in `Net::Socket::ReadRaw` or in RemoteBuild.op between those
builds, rather than anything in this repo's scripts.

## Fix, and who may make it

`HandleClient` must accumulate reads until `Json::Parse` succeeds (or a declared
length is satisfied). The user's fork is at
`~/src/openplanet/my-plugins/tm-remote-build`, same code as the installed `.op`.

**Coordinator ruling: an agent may implement this in the fork ON A BRANCH, with
the log-verified one-read check above. No agent installs it into the live game
or touches the installed `.op` — that is Max's call.**

## Related

B068 (batched-unit channel), B077 (the fail-closed teardown that is currently
containing this), B080 (its probe is blocked by this), B086 (cannot be freshly
probed today because of this).

## PROVENANCE OF THE EVIDENCE (recorded 2026-08-31, at lane-unused's insistence)

Worth pinning, because a ticket this consequential will be re-read by someone
deciding whether to trust it.

**The decisive evidence is a real run, not reasoning.** lane-unused probed with
an explicit coordinator grant at 18:48, staging
`lsp-issue-repro-lsp-b087-probe-zz-control-must-reject` (marker pid 2366987).
The harness staged, attempted `load_plugin`, and the load never reached the
server. The capture below is **the game's own log**, from that run:

    Bytes to read: 73
    Read payload: {"route":"load_plugin","data":{"id":"a","source":"user","type":"
    Bytes to read: 9
    Read payload: folder"}}

Two reads, two requests, two `{"error":"Not implemented: ","data":""}` responses
with an EMPTY route name, because neither fragment parsed.

lane-tooling then independently derived the exact threshold (66 OK / 67 FAIL,
3 trials each with a padded `get_status`) and identified the mechanism in
`HandleClient`. So this ticket rests on **two independent lanes: one live log
capture and one controlled boundary measurement**, which is why the concurrency
framing it was originally filed under could be discarded outright rather than
held as an alternative.

The fixture that run left behind was preserved by B077's fail-closed teardown
(`sweep_stale` correctly refused it as a non-empty marked directory) and was
removed by hand by the lane that owned it, after four checks: marker pid dead,
exactly the three files it staged, byte-identical to source, and ZERO mentions
of its symbols in the game log — i.e. it was never compiled. Plugins dir
verified back to 44 entries.

**That last check is the one worth copying.** "Never compiled" is not inferable
from the filesystem; it took reading the game's log for the fixture's own
symbols. A stranded fixture that WAS compiled is a contaminating installed
plugin; one that never compiled is only litter. Different severities, and only
the log distinguishes them.

## Corrected attribution

An earlier coordinator note on this ticket implied the staging came from an
unexplained code path in the probe harness — some path that stages before
deciding not to probe. **There is no such path.** The lane's "I never took the
game port" statement predated the grant reaching it (messages between sessions
are asynchronous, and it had composed that line before the grant landed); it
then probed with permission and documented the run. Nothing about the harness's
staging behaviour is unaccounted for.

## SECOND CORRECTION — the cap is 64, it PREDATES today's update, and load_plugin has NEVER worked

Measured by the tm-tournament-station session, single client, from
RemoteBuild's own logging of `Available()` alongside the payload it read:

    Bytes to read: 65  -> payload 64b
    Bytes to read: 70  -> payload 64b
    Bytes to read: 80  -> payload 64b
    Bytes to read: 58  -> payload 58b

`Available()` reports the true size; **`ReadRaw(n)` returns at most 64.**
Bisected exactly: <=64 fine, >=65 splits. `Main.as` hands each read straight to
`Router::Update`, which treats it as a whole message, so a split request gives
`Json::Parse` a truncated string -> Null -> route `""` ->
`{"error":"Not implemented: "}`. The remainder arrives next tick and fails the
same way.

**Two things I asserted on this ticket that are WRONG:**

1. **The threshold is 64, not "~64-66".** The earlier 66/67 boundary was
   measured with a padded `get_status` and is off by the framing bytes.
2. **This does NOT date from the 15:10 Openplanet update.** I attributed it to
   that and it was wrong — **`Openplanet-Old.log` caps at 64 as well.** So the
   fault predates today entirely, and **`load_plugin` has NEVER worked**: its
   minimum payload is 72 bytes with an EMPTY id. Anything anyone has ever seen
   loaded was loaded at game start, not by the call.

## THE CONTROL COULD NOT HAVE CAUGHT IT — the transferable part

At 18:54:52 nine routes were probed — `get_status`, `unload_plugin`, `unload`,
`reload_plugin`, `load_plugin`, `list_plugins`, `get_plugin`, and
`zzz_no_such_route` — every one carrying a long `lsp-issue-repro-lsp-b089-...`
id. **Every one logged exactly 64b and every one returned "Not implemented".**

`zzz_no_such_route` was the negative control, and **it failed through the same
mechanism as the positives.** A control that fails the way the test fails is not
a control; it is a second test. It cannot discriminate, which is the one job it
had.

**Consequence: any conclusion of the form "route X is unimplemented" drawn from
a payload over 64 bytes is manufactured evidence.** Retract any such finding.

The general form, for the hazards doc: **a negative control must fail for a
DIFFERENT reason than the positive case would.** A control sharing the failure
channel with the thing under test is indistinguishable from it, and its passing
"correctly" is what convinces you the instrument works.

## THE SHIPPED CORPORA ARE VALID — and B068 is why we can know it

This needed checking rather than assuming, because "load_plugin never worked"
initially looks like it invalidates every corpus in the repo. It does not.

`probe_enum_corpus.py:run_batch` stages the folder, calls `load_plugin`, and
**IGNORES the result**, sleeps 3s, then parses the game's log window. It is
**Openplanet's own file watcher**, not the route call, that compiles a staged
plugin folder. The route call has always been decoration.

And a non-compile CANNOT masquerade as success, because of B068's sentinel:
`attribute()` sets `analysis_reached_the_end = sentinel_line in by_line`, and
without it every name with no diagnostic is `None` / UNDETERMINED rather than
`''` / ACCEPTED. It also requires `d.get("file") == source_file`, so another
plugin's errors cannot be misattributed.

**Today's re-probe returned 742 verdicts with ZERO undetermined**, which is only
possible if the probe plugin genuinely compiled. So b052, b053 and the B068
re-probe all stand.

Worth stating for the record: B068 was filed as a batching-attribution defect,
and its sentinel turns out to be the thing that lets us answer a completely
different question — "did this plugin compile at all?" — six weeks of assumption
later. A guard that distinguishes *analysed and silent* from *never analysed*
is more general than the defect that motivated it.

`issue_repro_game.py` is the path that genuinely breaks: its teardown goes
through the B077 holder, the unload fails, the fixture is preserved, and the run
is marked SKIP.

## WORKING ALTERNATIVE — no restart needed

`tm-control-mcp` is loaded and its socket reassembles correctly. RemoteBuild
parity (enable / disable / setEnabled / reload / unload / load / openSettings /
getLogs) plus ListPlugins / GetPlugin:

    cd ~/src/openplanet/my-plugins/tm-control-mcp
    python3 tools/call.py ControlPlugin '{"id":"<plugin>","action":"load"}'
    python3 tools/call.py ControlPlugin '{"id":"<plugin>","action":"reload"}'

It returns the compile log **inline**, with lines attributed to the build you
triggered. That second property independently fixes a different hazard this repo
has hit: collecting diagnostics from the shared log by severity and picking up a
foreign plugin's errors.

**Probe tooling should migrate to this rather than wait on a RemoteBuild fix.**

